/K [7]
>>
endobj
1107 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 364 0 R
/T (Overview : How Data Flows Through the System)
/E (Overview : How Data Flows Through the System)
/Pg 58 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1108 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 364 0 R
/E (Every processing cycle starts with the StreamManager pulling the latest frame from each camera. These frames go to the detector as a single batched GPU call , not one camera at a time, all of them together. The detector returns bounding boxes and confidence scores. High-confidence detections go to OSNet for appearance encoding. Low-confidence ones are kept aside as motion anchors.)
/Pg 58 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1109 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 364 0 R
/E (Each camera has its own SingleCameraTracker instance. The tracker predicts where existing tracks should be, runs the matching cascade, updates confirmed tracks with new measurements, and emits state changes as events. Once per cycle, the GlobalMatcher collects the active tracks from all cameras and checks whether anyone on Camera 2 matches a known identity from Camera 1.)
/Pg 58 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1110 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 364 0 R
/E (Confirmed events , appearances, re-identifications, losses , go to the Orchestrator, which routes them to the frontend over Socket.IO and triggers alerts where needed. The frontend updates live. The whole cycle runs continuously, camera to dashboard, with no human intervention required.)
/Pg 58 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1111 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 364 0 R
/E (The key throughput decision is batched GPU inference. With four cameras running, naively processing them sequentially would mean eight GPU calls per cycle , four for detection, four for encoding. Batching collapses that to two. At four cameras, this alone saves roughly 18ms per cycle from kernel-launch overhead, independent of what the models actually compute. At scale, it matters.)
/Pg 58 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1112 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 364 0 R
/Pg 58 0 R
/A [2278 0 R 0]
/K [5]
>>
endobj
